<!-- Added by Plutext Pty Ltd for the docx4j FO renderer (docx4j-fo-renderer), a modified distribution derived
     from Apache FOP 2.11: hook float-table-split, a floating table the page cannot hold below its anchor split across
     pages as a word processor splits it (fop/CR-034). -->
<testcase>
  <info>
    <p>
      A side float whose content is a table, and which the page cannot hold below its anchor, is split across pages
      as Word splits a floating table (hook float-table-split, fop/CR-034): the rows that fit are drawn on the anchor's
      page from the float's top with nothing beside them, further pages take whole parts, and the anchor's paragraph
      opens the page where the table ends, beside its last rows. A 400pt page with 50pt margins, a 300pt body, 10pt
      text on 12pt lines, a 250pt-wide table of 12.5pt rows leaving 50pt beside it. First sequence: a 30-row table
      anchored after two lines (its top 24pt down), 22 rows on page 1 and the anchor beside rows 23 to 30 on page 2.
      Second: the same with fox:float-offset 12pt, 21 rows from 36pt down, the continuation at the top of page 2
      (the offset holds on the first page only). Third: a header row, repeated on the continuation. Fourth: the anchor
      opens the flow, 24 rows on page 1. Fifth: 100 rows over five pages, nothing beside the first four parts. Sixth:
      a row of thirty lines split inside itself. Seventh: a kept row of twenty lines after four lines, moved whole. Eighth: a float
      that is no table is not split. Word's float-foot-crossing, float-tall-beside and float-three-pages probes
      (2026-11-03).
    </p>
  </info>
